 ## Overview
-This method extracts robust, generalizable per-pixel features from input RGB images to serve as a compact description of the light ray emitted from the camera center to the 3D surface [S1]. It solves the problem of raw RGB values being sensitive to lighting conditions and environmental noise by learning higher-level patterns [S1]. It differs from sibling methods like [ResNet Architecture](8378-resnet-architecture.md) by explicitly stacking camera position data to each pixel to encode 3D spatial awareness, and from [Attention-based Multi-View Density Prediction](10674-attention-based-multi-view-density-prediction.md) by relying on convolutional skip connections rather than attention mechanisms for feature fusion [S1].
+This method uses a convolutional neural network (CNN) to extract robust, per-pixel feature vectors from input RGB images, replacing raw RGB values which are sensitive to lighting and noise [S1]. It differs from simple raw pixel usage by learning general patterns and explicitly incorporating camera position awareness, while differing from other spatial encodings by focusing on 2D image-space feature maps that are later reprojected to 3D [S1]. It builds on [Spatial Feature Encoding](16657-spatial-feature-encoding.md).
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A set of input RGB images with known camera poses [S1]. Each image has resolution $H \times W$ [S1]. The camera pose provides the 3D location (xyz) of the camera center [S1].
-**Outputs:** A feature map of size $H \times W \times 128$ containing robust per-pixel features [S1].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** An RGB image of size $H \times W \times 3$ and the camera's 3D location (xyz) vector $\mathbf{c} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ [S1].
+*   **Output:** A feature map $F \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times D}$, where $D$ is the feature dimension (e.g., 128) [S1].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-The input tensor is constructed by stacking the camera xyz location to each pixel of the input RGB image [S1].
-*   Input Tensor Shape: $H \times W \times (3 + 3)$, where the first 3 channels are RGB values and the next 3 channels are the duplicated camera xyz coordinates [S1].
-*   Output Tensor Shape: $H \times W \times 128$ [S1].
+The input tensor is augmented by stacking the camera position. For each pixel $p$ at coordinates $(u, v)$, the input feature vector $\mathbf{x}_{u,v}$ is constructed as:
+$$ \mathbf{x}_{u,v} = [\text{RGB}_{u,v}, \mathbf{c}] \in \mathbb{R}^{3+3} $$
+The resulting input tensor has dimensions $H \times W \times 6$ [S1].
 
-**Algorithm Steps:**
-1.  **Preprocessing:** For each input image, duplicate the camera's 3D position (xyz) and append it as additional channels to every pixel's RGB vector [S1]. This creates an input tensor of dimension $H \times W \times 6$ [S1].
-2.  **Encoding:** Pass the preprocessed tensor through a convolutional encoder [S1]. The encoder uses skip connections to preserve high-frequency local features [S1].
-3.  **Global Feature Learning (Optional):** Integrate fully connected (FC) layers in the middle of the CNN module to learn global features [S1]. This creates a mixture of hierarchical features that is more general and representative [S1].
-4.  **Decoding:** Pass the encoded features through a convolutional decoder, utilizing skip connections from the encoder to restore spatial detail [S1].
-5.  **Output:** Produce the final per-pixel feature map [S1].
+**Steps:**
+1.  **Input Augmentation:** Duplicate the camera position vector $\mathbf{c}$ across all $H \times W$ pixels and concatenate it with the RGB channels [S1].
+2.  **Convolutional Encoding:** Pass the augmented tensor through an encoder-decoder CNN architecture [S1].
+3.  **Skip Connections:** Apply skip connections between the encoder and decoder stages to preserve high-frequency local features [S1].
+4.  **Global Feature Learning (Optional):** Integrate fully connected (FC) layers in the middle of the network to learn global features [S1].
+5.  **Output Generation:** Produce the final feature map $F$ [S1].
 
